   929	probability at least $1/2$ for large $C$.  After a failed attempt, confinement
   930	and \eqref{eq:deficit-return} return the process to $R\leq R_0$ in
   931	polynomial time; unless fixation has already occurred, the hub is activated
   932	again at a rate bounded below.  The strong Markov property over
   933	$O_{B_0}(\log C)$ such attempts gives failure $O(C^{-B_0})$, while their
   934	total duration is polynomial.  Taking $D_{\rm cl}$ larger than the two block
   935	exponents and using \eqref{eq:core-confinement} proves the first assertion.
   936	
   937	For a Bd mutant leaf, use the standard weighted graphical construction: each
   938	vertex $u$ has a base reproduction clock of rate one and, when it rings,
   939	targets $v$ with probability $w_{uv}/d_u$; a mutant $u$ has an independent
   940	extra clock of rate $r-1$ with the same target law.  Deleting mutants can
   941	only decrease the mutant set.  Fix the original mutant pendant.  At every
   942	regeneration with resident hub, no ordinary mutants, and that pendant still
   943	alive, delete all other mutants and suppress births to the other pendants.
   944	This produces the adverse lower state $(h,i,\ell)=(0,0,1)$.
   945	In its resident-hub phase, activation has rate $r$ and loss of the
   946	distinguished pendant has rate $1/d$, so loss before activation is
   947	$O(C^{-1})$.  In the ensuing mutant-hub phase, an ordinary seed has rate at
   948	least $rc/d\geq a_0$, while hub loss has rate $m$; hence a seed precedes hub
   949	loss with probability at least $a_1/m$.
   950	
   951	After a seed, stop the ordinary count on $\{0,K\}$.  For every hidden hub
   952	and pendant state, the Bd up/down odds on $1\leq i<K$ are at least a fixed
   953	$r_1>1$.  The conditional-bias argument therefore gives
   954	$\Prob_1(\tau_K<\tau_0)\geq p_0>0$.  The embedded walk makes $O(K)$ expected
   955	changes, and its changing intensity is bounded below, so
   956	$\mathbb E\tau_{0,K}=O(K)$.  The distinguished-pendant loss intensity is at
   957	most $1/d$, giving $O(K/C)$ loss during this stopped trial.  If the count
   958	hits zero while the pendant survives, wait for resident-hub regeneration,
   959	suppressing or deleting every ordinary reseed and every additional mutant
   960	pendant until hub loss.  This is again an adverse graphical coupling: the
   961	distinguished pendant cannot be lost while its sole neighbor is mutant, and
   962	any subsequent resident-phase loss is charged as trial failure.  Delete the
   963	extra mutants again at regeneration.  If it hits $K$, the preceding completion
   964	argument fixes with probability $1-o(1)$.  Thus a trial succeeds with
   965	probability at least $a_2/m$, whereas it loses the distinguished pendant
   966	with probability $O((1+K)/C)$.  The elementary renewal inequality for these
   967	competing outcomes gives loss before success
   968	$O(m(1+K)/C)=o(1)$.  Hence $u_{\rm leaf}^{\Bd}(r)=1-o(1)$.
   969	Under dB, the leaf dies at rate one after the common death-clock time change,
   970	whereas hub activation is at most $r_+/C$.  Fixation requires that activation,
   971	so $u_{\rm leaf}^{\dB}(r)=O(C^{-1})$.
   972	\end{proof}
   973	
   974	\begin{lemma}[Reciprocal killed-Green bounds]
   975	\label{lem:killed-green}
   976	Put $s=1/r$ and suppress hub changes while $h=0,\ell=0$.  Stop the resulting
   977	ordinary-count chain $I$ on hitting $0$ or $\lceil\delta c\rceil$, and define
   978	\[
   979	 G_C^U(i,j)=\mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau
   980	             \mathbf1_{\{I_z=j\}}\,dz.
   981	\]
   982	For every fixed $L$ there are $a_L,\kappa,\theta>0$, uniform for
   983	$s\in[1/r_+,1/r_-]$ and $U\in\{\Bd,\dB\}$, such that
   984	\begin{align}
   985	 \sup_{i\leq L}\Prob_i(\tau_{\lceil\delta c\rceil}<\tau_0)
   986	 &\leq a_Le^{-\kappa C},\label{eq:green-macro}\\
   987	 \sup_{i\leq L}\sum_jjG_C^U(i,j)&\leq a_L,
   988	 \label{eq:green-mass}\\
   989	 \sup_{i\leq L}\sum_{j>L'}jG_C^U(i,j)
   990	 &\leq a_Le^{-\theta L'}.
   991	 \label{eq:green-tail}
   992	\end{align}
   993	Consequently the probability of a hub change before ordinary extinction is
   994	$O_L(C^{-1})$, and the ordinary count at that change has an exponentially
   995	decreasing tail after multiplication by $C$.
   996	\end{lemma}
   997	
   998	\begin{proof}
   999	For $i\leq\delta c$, the reciprocal-fitness versions of the two rate tables
  1000	give
  1001	\[
  1002	 \frac{q_i^+}{q_i^-}\leq s_+\{1+O(\delta)+O(C^{-1})\}\leq\vartheta<1
  1003	\]
  1004	after decreasing $\delta$, where $s_+=1/r_-$.  They also give
  1005	$q_i^--q_i^+\geq\kappa_0i$ in the displayed clock.  The product-odds formula
  1006	for the dominated nearest-neighbor walk proves \eqref{eq:green-macro}.
  1007	Dynkin's formula for $I_{z\wedge\tau}$ gives
  1008	\[
  1009	 \kappa_0\mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau I_z\,dz\leq i,
  1010	\]
  1011	which is \eqref{eq:green-mass}.  To obtain \eqref{eq:green-tail}, let $\nu$
  1012	be the first hit of $L'+1$.  Conditional bias gives
  1013	$\Prob_i(\nu<\tau_0)\leq K_L\vartheta^{L'-L}$.  After $\nu$, the same Dynkin
  1014	estimate bounds the expected remaining particle-time by
  1015	$(L'+1)/\kappa_0$.  Therefore
  1016	\[
  1017	 \mathbb E_i\int_0^\tau I_s\mathbf1_{\{I_s>L'\}}\,ds
  1018	 \leq \frac{K_L(L'+1)}{\kappa_0}\vartheta^{L'-L}
  1019	 \leq a_Le^{-\theta L'},
  1020	\]
