  1182	\section{Pair gates and the complete post-establishment path}
  1183	
  1184	For the center, let
  1185	\[
  1186	 I_H=\sum_{x\ {\rm in\ clique}}\frac1{d_x}
  1187	 =1+\frac1{C-1+m},
  1188	\qquad
  1189	 J_H(r)=\sum_{x\ {\rm in\ clique}}
  1190	 \frac{h_H^{\dB}(\{x\};r)}{d_x},
  1191	\]
  1192	where $h_H^{\dB}$ is the isolated-center dB fixation committor.
  1193	For a pair with internal weight $W=C/\sigma$, put
  1194	\[
  1195	 I_P=\frac2W=\frac{2\sigma}{C},\qquad
  1196	 J_P(r)=\frac1W=\frac{\sigma}{C}.
  1197	\]
  1198	The second identity uses the exact dB singleton value $1/2$ on $K_2$.
  1199	The center estimates imply
  1200	\[
  1201	 I_H=1+o(1),\qquad J_H(r)=p+o(1),
  1202	\]
  1203	while $J_H(1/r)$ and the reciprocal core fixation probability are
  1204	$o(C^{-1})$.
  1205	
  1206	\begin{proposition}[Gate odds and global sweep]
  1207	\label{prop:gate}
  1208	For every fixed $r>1$, the separated macro-fixation probabilities satisfy
  1209	\[
  1210	 P_U^H=1-o(q/C),
  1211	 \qquad
  1212	 P_{\Bd}^P\longrightarrow\frac{Z_B}{1+Z_B},
  1213	 \qquad
  1214	 P_{\dB}^P\longrightarrow\frac{Z_D}{1+Z_D},
  1215	\]
  1216	where
  1217	\[
  1218	 Z_B=\sigma(r^2-1),\qquad
  1219	 Z_D=\frac{2r(r-1)}{\sigma}.
  1220	\]
  1221	The convergence is uniform when $r$ ranges over a compact subinterval of
  1222	$(1,\infty)$.
  1223	\end{proposition}
  1224	
  1225	\begin{proof}
  1226	For one specified pair, let $A,D,B,C'$ denote, respectively, mutant-pair
  1227	invasion of a resident center, resident-center recovery of that pair,
  1228	mutant-center invasion of a resident pair, and resident-pair recovery of
  1229	the center.  In each row below $a_P$ means the isolated pair fixation
  1230	$a_P^U$ for that update rule.  Summing the parent--target or death--parent events gives,
  1231	after a common time change,
  1232	\[
  1233	\begin{array}{c|cccc}
  1234	 &A&D&B&C'\\ \hline
  1235	 \Bd&
  1236	 CrI_Pu_{\rm core}^{\Bd}(r)&
  1237	 2I_Ha_P(1/r)&
  1238	 2rI_Ha_P(r)&
  1239	 CI_Pu_{\rm core}^{\Bd}(1/r)\\[1mm]
  1240	 \dB&
  1241	 2rJ_H(r)&
  1242	 (C/r)J_P(1/r)&
  1243	 CrJ_P(r)&
  1244	 (2/r)J_H(1/r).
  1245	\end{array}
  1246	\]
  1247	The isolated pair values are
  1248	\[
  1249	 a_P^{\Bd}(r)=\frac r{r+1},\quad
  1250	 a_P^{\Bd}(1/r)=\frac1{r+1},\quad
  1251	 a_P^{\dB}(r)=a_P^{\dB}(1/r)=\frac12.
  1252	\]
  1253	
  1254	On macro states the only nonzero rates are
  1255	\[
  1256	\begin{array}{ll}
  1257	 (0,k)\to(1,k):kA,&(0,k)\to(0,k-1):kD,\\
  1258	 (1,k)\to(1,k+1):(q-k)B,&(1,k)\to(0,k):(q-k)C'.
  1259	\end{array}
  1260	\]
  1261	From $(1,k)$, the chance that the center reverses before the next pair
  1262	conversion is $C'/(B+C')$.  A union bound over at most $q$ conversions gives
  1263	\[
  1264	 1-P_U^H\le q\frac{C'}{B+C'}.
  1265	\]
  1266	Proposition~\ref{prop:center} and the portal estimates give
  1267	$qC'/B=o(q/C)$, uniformly on each fitness compact.  Thus a mutant center
  1268	converts all resident pairs with probability $1-o(q/C)$.
  1269	
  1270	From $(0,1)$, the first successful macro event is either center invasion at
  1271	rate $A$ or loss of the sole mutant pair at rate $D$.  Hence
  1272	\[
  1273	 P_U^P=\frac{A}{A+D}\,p_{1,1},
  1274	\]
  1275	where $p_{1,1}$ is the macro-fixation probability from $(1,1)$.  The same
  1276	sweep bound, now with at most $q-1$ remaining conversions, gives
  1277	$p_{1,1}\to1$.  (Equivalently,
  1278	$P_U^P=[A/(A+D)][(B+C')/B]P_U^H$.)
  1279	The rate table and the isolated $K_2$ values give
  1280	\[
  1281	 Z_B=\sigma(r^2-1),\qquad
  1282	 Z_D=\frac{2r(r-1)}{\sigma}.
  1283	\]
  1284	More precisely, $A/D\to Z_U$, which yields the stated limits.  The argument
  1285	uses the exact absorbing macro chain, including adverse reversals; no
  1286	branching survival probability is substituted for global fixation.
  1287	\end{proof}
  1288	
  1289	\section{The simultaneous correction and its optimization}
  1290	
  1291	Put $\eta=q/C$.  Substitution of
  1292	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB} and
  1293	Proposition~\ref{prop:gate} into the finite trace gives the key asymptotic
  1294	statement.
  1295	
  1296	\begin{proposition}[Two response functions]
  1297	\label{prop:responses}
  1298	For $C=t^4$, $q=t$, $m/q\to\lambda$, and fixed $\sigma>0$, the separated
  1299	trace satisfies
  1300	\begin{align}
  1301	 \frac{\rho^0_{\Bd,t}(r)}{\rho_{\Bd}(K_{n_t},r)}
  1302	 &=1+\eta\left[
  1303	 \frac{2(\sigma-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)}
  1304	 +\frac{\lambda}{r-1}\right]+o_r(\eta),\label{eq:B}\\
  1305	 \frac{\rho^0_{\dB,t}(r)}{\rho_{\dB}(K_{n_t},r)}
  1306	 &=1+\eta\left[
  1307	 \frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}
  1308	 -\lambda\right]+o_r(\eta).\label{eq:D}
  1309	\end{align}
  1310	The remainders are uniform on every compact subinterval of $(1,\infty)$.
  1311	\end{proposition}
  1312	
  1313	\begin{proof}
  1314	The complete-graph Bd baseline differs from $p$ exponentially in $C$, while
  1315	the dB baseline differs from $p$ by $O(C^{-1})$; both errors are
  1316	$o(\eta)$.  The center terms follow from
  1317	\eqref{eq:center-Bd}--\eqref{eq:center-dB}.  The pair terms follow by the
  1318	exact simplifications
  1319	\begin{align}
  1320	 2\left\{\frac{[r/(r+1)]Z_B/(1+Z_B)}p-1\right\}
  1321	 &=\frac{2(\sigma-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)},\\
  1322	 2\left\{\frac{[1/2]Z_D/(1+Z_D)}p-1\right\}
  1323	 &=\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}.
  1324	\end{align}
  1325	One leaf replaces one core vertex and contributes
  1326	$1/p-1=1/(r-1)$ under Bd and $-1$ under dB.
  1327	All inputs are compact-uniform by Propositions~\ref{prop:weak-trace},
  1328	\ref{prop:center}, and~\ref{prop:gate}, so the combined remainders are as
  1329	stated.
  1330	\end{proof}
  1331	
  1332	\begin{lemma}[Exact tangency]
  1333	\label{lem:tangency}
  1334	At $(\sigma_*,\lambda_*)$, both response functions in
  1335	\eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D} are strictly positive for every
  1336	$1<r<\Rhyb$ and vanish simultaneously at $r=\Rhyb$.
  1337	\end{lemma}
  1338	
  1339	\begin{proof}
  1340	Both bracketed corrections are positive precisely when
  1341	\[
  1342	 L(r,\sigma)<\lambda<U(r,\sigma),
  1343	\]
  1344	where
  1345	\[
  1346	 L=\frac{2(1-\sigma)(r-1)}{1+\sigma(r^2-1)},\qquad
  1347	 U=\frac{2\{r(2-r)-\sigma\}}{\sigma+2r(r-1)}.
  1348	\]
  1349	After multiplication by positive denominators, $L<U$ is equivalent to
  1350	\[
  1351	 F_r(\sigma)<0,
  1352	\]
  1353	where
  1354	\[
  1355	 F_r(\sigma)
  1356	 =(r-1)\sigma^2+(r^3-4r^2+3r+1)\sigma+r(2r-3).
  1357	\]
  1358	Its minimizing value and minimum are
  1359	\[
  1360	 \sigma(r)=\frac{-r^3+4r^2-3r-1}{2(r-1)},\qquad
  1361	 \min_\sigma F_r(\sigma)=-\frac{P(r)}{4(r-1)}.
  1362	\]
  1363	Exact Sturm counting gives no root of $P$ in $(1,3/2)$ and exactly one in
  1364	$(3/2,151/100)$.  At that root, $\sigma_*$ is the double tangency and
  1365	\[
  1366	 L(\Rhyb,\sigma_*)=U(\Rhyb,\sigma_*)=\lambda_*.
  1367	\]
  1368	For fixed $\sigma_*$, $L$ is increasing and $U$ decreasing on
  1369	$(1,\Rhyb)$ because
  1370	\[
  1371	 \partial_rL=
  1372	 \frac{2(\sigma-1)\{\sigma(r-1)^2-1\}}
  1373	      {\{1+\sigma(r^2-1)\}^2}>0,\qquad
  1374	 \partial_rU=
  1375	 \frac{4r(\sigma-r)}{\{2r(r-1)+\sigma\}^2}<0.
  1376	\]
  1377	Here $0<\sigma_*<1<r$ and
  1378	$\sigma_*(r-1)^2<1$ throughout the isolating interval, so the displayed
  1379	factorizations determine the signs without numerical approximation.
  1380	Thus
  1381	\[
  1382	 L(r,\sigma_*)<\lambda_*<U(r,\sigma_*)
  1383	 \quad(1<r<\Rhyb),
  1384	\]
  1385	which proves the claim.
  1386	\end{proof}
  1387	
  1388	\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}]
  1389	Fix $r\in(1,\Rhyb)$.  Then $r\in I_t$ for every sufficiently large $t$.
  1390	By Proposition~\ref{prop:responses} and Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency}, the two
  1391	separated normalized gains, after multiplication by $n_t/q_t$, converge to
  1392	strictly positive numbers.  The defining inequality for $e_t$ changes each
  1393	scaled gain by at most $1/t$.  Thus both actual connected-graph gains are
  1394	strictly positive for all sufficiently large $t$.  The sequence, including
  1395	every weak-edge weight, was fixed before $r$ was chosen, which is exactly the
  1396	quantifier order in the definition of $\Rsim$.
  1397	\end{proof}
  1398	
  1399	\begin{proposition}[Fixed-parameter response optimality]
  1400	\label{prop:fixed-response-optimal}
  1401	Among fixed positive $(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed first-order dilute
  1402	pair--pendant response model \eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D}, $\Rhyb$ is the exact
  1403	largest threshold of an interval beginning at one on which both corrections
  1404	can remain positive.
  1405	\end{proposition}
  1406	
  1407	\begin{proof}
  1408	For fixed positive $\sigma$, feasibility implies $F_r(\sigma)<0$, and hence
  1409	$\min_{s\in\mathbb R}F_r(s)<0$.  At $\Rhyb$ the unconstrained minimum is
  1410	zero at the admissible value $0<\sigma_*<1$.  Exact evaluation gives
  1411	\[
  1412	 P(3/2)=\frac1{64}>0,\qquad
  1413	 P(151/100)=-\frac{39866792399}{10^{12}}<0.
  1414	\]
  1415	The Sturm count in Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency} shows that $\Rhyb$ is the only
  1416	root between these endpoints.  Hence $P(r)<0$ on
  1417	$(\Rhyb,151/100]$ and
  1418	$\min_sF_r(s)=-P(r)/\{4(r-1)\}>0$ there.  No positive $\sigma$---indeed no
  1419	real $\sigma$---is feasible immediately beyond $\Rhyb$.  Since the fixed
  1420	pair $(\sigma_*,\lambda_*)$ works throughout $(1,\Rhyb)$, no interval
  1421	beginning at one can extend farther within this response model.
  1422	\end{proof}
  1423	
  1424	\begin{corollary}[A rational-edge family]
  1425	\label{cor:rational}
  1426	Taking $\sigma=19/137$ and $\lambda=20/27$ gives, exactly at $r=3/2$,
  1427	\[
  1428	 B=\frac{232}{17361}>0,\qquad
  1429	 D=\frac{65}{12123}>0.
  1430	\]
  1431	This choice gives a family with entirely rational edge weights and algebraic
  1432	response threshold
  1433	\[
  1434	 R_{\mathbb Q}
  1435	 =\frac{5069+12\sqrt{147001}}{6439}
  1436	 =1.50176815223369\ldots>3/2.
  1437	\]
  1438	Thus a family with rational internal ratios and rational positive weak edges
  1439	already proves a strict interval beyond $3/2$.
  1440	\end{corollary}
  1441	
  1442	\begin{proof}
  1443	Substitution into \eqref{eq:B}--\eqref{eq:D} gives the two displayed
  1444	margins.  For these rational parameters the dB response first vanishes at
  1445	the unique root in $(1,2)$ of
  1446	\[
  1447	 6439r^2-10138r+703=0,
  1448	\]
  1449	which is $R_{\mathbb Q}$; the Bd response is still strictly positive there.
  1450	Both responses are therefore positive on $(1,R_{\mathbb Q})$ by the same
  1451	monotonicity calculation used in Lemma~\ref{lem:tangency}.  The weak edges
  1452	remain rational as well: repeat the least-dyadic diagonal construction with
  1453	$I_t=[1+1/t,R_{\mathbb Q}-1/t]$, $\sigma=19/137$, and
  1454	$m_t=\lfloor(20/27)t\rfloor$.  Every edge-weight ratio in this specialized
  1455	family is therefore rational.
  1456	\end{proof}
  1457	
  1458	\section{Discussion}
  1459	
  1460	The central contribution is the quantifier in Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.  One
  1461	weighted graph sequence is chosen without knowing fitness and works for each
  1462	fixed $r$ in an interval extending beyond $3/2$.  A family tuned separately
  1463	at each fitness would establish a weaker statement.  The diagonal choice of
  1464	$\eps_t$ is therefore part of the construction, not merely a technical
  1465	limit: compact convergence selects one positive cut that works
  1466	simultaneously on an expanding interval of fitnesses.
  1467	
  1468	The mechanism uses two modules with opposite response signatures.  Heavy
  1469	pairs contribute positively to dB and negatively to Bd, whereas hub
  1470	pendants contribute positively to Bd and negatively to dB.  Their densities
  1471	are vanishing, so the well-mixed core supplies the baseline and the module
  1472	responses appear at the common scale $q_t/C_t$.  The exact Schur trace is
  1473	important here.  It resolves every local excursion before the next weak-cut
  1474	event and retains the possibility that a nearly successful global sweep
  1475	reverses; replacing it by an independent-lineage approximation would not
  1476	prove fixation.
  1477	
  1478	Biologically, Bd and dB idealize two different ways local turnover can be
  1479	resolved: reproduction can select a parent before a replacement site, or a
  1480	vacancy can first determine which neighbors compete.  The construction
  1481	shows that robustness to this order need not require most individuals to
  1482	occupy a special environment.  A vanishing fraction of paired niches and
  1483	hub-associated peripheral sites can bias invasion in complementary ways
  1484	while the bulk remains well mixed.  The result is qualitative rather than a
  1485	claim of empirical optimality: it identifies a mechanism by which rare
  1486	structural heterogeneities can overcome update-order antagonism in this
  1487	beneficial-fitness regime.
  1488	
  1489	There are two sharp limitations.  Proposition~\ref{prop:fixed-response-optimal}
  1490	optimizes only fixed positive $(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed
  1491	first-order dilute response model.  It does not classify size-dependent or
  1492	singular choices $\sigma_t,\lambda_t$, different pair or pendant density
  1493	scales, second-order gains on a vanishing first-order boundary, nonseparated
  1494	pair dynamics, other modules, or non-dilute interactions.  Conversely, the
  1495	paper supplies no finite universal upper bound on $\Rsim$.  Establishing
  1496	such a bound would require a comparison that couples Bd and dB fixation on
  1497	arbitrary growing weighted graphs, rather than a classification of this
  1498	construction.
  1499	
  1500	The quantitative advantage is also asymptotically small.  Here
  1501	$q_t/C_t=t^{-3}=\Theta(n_t^{-3/4})$, so the normalized fixation gain tends to
  1502	zero.  The pair weights grow like $C_t$, while the exact diagonal may select
  1503	very small positive weak edges.  The proof gives eventual positivity for
  1504	each fixed fitness but no useful finite-size bound on $t_0(r)$, and it makes
  1505	no claim of empirical or finite-population optimality.
  1506	
  1507	\section*{Statements and Declarations}
