  1458	\section{Discussion}
  1459	
  1460	The central contribution is the quantifier in Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.  One
  1461	weighted graph sequence is chosen without knowing fitness and works for each
  1462	fixed $r$ in an interval extending beyond $3/2$.  A family tuned separately
  1463	at each fitness would establish a weaker statement.  The diagonal choice of
  1464	$\eps_t$ is therefore part of the construction, not merely a technical
  1465	limit: compact convergence selects one positive cut that works
  1466	simultaneously on an expanding interval of fitnesses.
  1467	
  1468	The mechanism uses two modules with opposite response signatures.  Heavy
  1469	pairs contribute positively to dB and negatively to Bd, whereas hub
  1470	pendants contribute positively to Bd and negatively to dB.  Their densities
  1471	are vanishing, so the well-mixed core supplies the baseline and the module
  1472	responses appear at the common scale $q_t/C_t$.  The exact Schur trace is
  1473	important here.  It resolves every local excursion before the next weak-cut
  1474	event and retains the possibility that a nearly successful global sweep
  1475	reverses; replacing it by an independent-lineage approximation would not
  1476	prove fixation.
  1477	
  1478	Biologically, Bd and dB idealize two different ways local turnover can be
  1479	resolved: reproduction can select a parent before a replacement site, or a
  1480	vacancy can first determine which neighbors compete.  The construction
  1481	shows that robustness to this order need not require most individuals to
  1482	occupy a special environment.  A vanishing fraction of paired niches and
  1483	hub-associated peripheral sites can bias invasion in complementary ways
  1484	while the bulk remains well mixed.  The result is qualitative rather than a
  1485	claim of empirical optimality: it identifies a mechanism by which rare
  1486	structural heterogeneities can overcome update-order antagonism in this
  1487	beneficial-fitness regime.
  1488	
  1489	There are two sharp limitations.  Proposition~\ref{prop:fixed-response-optimal}
  1490	optimizes only fixed positive $(\sigma,\lambda)$ in the displayed
  1491	first-order dilute response model.  It does not classify size-dependent or
  1492	singular choices $\sigma_t,\lambda_t$, different pair or pendant density
  1493	scales, second-order gains on a vanishing first-order boundary, nonseparated
  1494	pair dynamics, other modules, or non-dilute interactions.  Conversely, the
  1495	paper supplies no finite universal upper bound on $\Rsim$.  Establishing
  1496	such a bound would require a comparison that couples Bd and dB fixation on
  1497	arbitrary growing weighted graphs, rather than a classification of this
  1498	construction.
  1499	
  1500	The quantitative advantage is also asymptotically small.  Here
  1501	$q_t/C_t=t^{-3}=\Theta(n_t^{-3/4})$, so the normalized fixation gain tends to
  1502	zero.  The pair weights grow like $C_t$, while the exact diagonal may select
  1503	very small positive weak edges.  The proof gives eventual positivity for
  1504	each fixed fitness but no useful finite-size bound on $t_0(r)$, and it makes
  1505	no claim of empirical or finite-population optimality.
  1506	
