   12                                                 Abstract
   13          We study fixation from a uniformly random single mutant on finite connected loopless undi-
   14      rected weighted graphs under both Birth–death (Bd) and death–Birth (dB) Moran updating.
   15      Let Rsim be the supremum of the fitness intervals (1, R) on which one graph family, chosen
   16      independently of fitness, eventually amplifies fixation under both rules. Let Rhyb be the unique
   17      root in (3/2, 151/100) of
   18 
   19                                R6 − 8R5 + 22R4 − 30R3 + 21R2 − 6R + 1.
   20 
   21      We construct one fitness-independent family that simultaneously amplifies every fixed 1 <
   22      r < Rhyb for all suﬀiciently large members of the sequence. Therefore Rsim ≥ Rhyb =
   23      1.5028569127905696 . . . > 3/2.
   24         The construction combines a large clique with dilute hub pendants and dilute, internally
   25      heavy two-vertex satellites. An exact finite-state derivation of the weak-cut limiting trace,
   26      together with uniform establishment, cleanup, and reciprocal-invasion estimates at the scale of
   27      the eventual gain, reduces the two normalized fixation corrections to explicit response functions.
   28      Optimizing their common positivity interval yields the displayed sextic. The same analysis
   29      proves that Rhyb is optimal among fixed positive (σ, λ) in the displayed first-order dilute pair–
   30      pendant response model; a specialization with entirely rational edge weights already crosses 3/2.
   31      Thus 3/2 is not the exact simultaneous-amplification threshold. The unrestricted value of Rsim ,
   32      and even a finite universal upper bound, remain open.
   33 
   34 Keywords: evolutionary graph theory; Moran process; fixation probability; simultaneous amplifi-
   35 cation; Birth–death updating; death–Birth updating.
   36 Mathematics Subject Classification (2020): 92D15, 60J10, 05C81.
