   39 1   Introduction
   40 Evolutionary graph theory extends the finite-population Moran process by representing sites as
   41 vertices and replacement opportunities as weighted edges (Moran, 1958; Lieberman et al., 2005;
   42 Allen et al., 2017). The order of the two elementary Moran events matters: stars and many other
   43 structures that amplify selection when birth precedes death suppress it when death precedes birth
   44 (Hindersin and Traulsen, 2015; Allen et al., 2020). Random edge weights also tend to make undi-
   45 rected networks less amplifying, and often suppressing, which emphasizes that useful weighting is
   46 
   47 
   48                                                      1
   49 a design problem rather than a generic effect (Bhaumik and Masuda, 2024). Death–Birth ampli-
   50 fication is necessarily transient in fitness on every fixed noncomplete strongly connected structure
   51 (Tkadlec et al., 2020), and transient dB amplifiers have also been sought through guided spectral
   52 edge deletion (Richter, 2023). A distinct approach randomizes the update order within a single
   53 mixed Moran process (Brewster et al., 2026). Here robustness instead means that the same graph
   54 beats the well-mixed benchmark under each pure rule separately. These tensions make robust-
   55 ness to update order a natural design problem rather than an automatic consequence of ordinary
   56 amplification.
   57     A population structure is a simultaneous amplifier at fitness r when it raises uniformly initialized
   58 fixation probability, relative to the complete graph of the same order, under both Bd and dB
   59 updating. The asymptotic problem has the quantifier order
   60 
   61                                  ∃{Gt } ∀r ∈ (1, R) ∃t0 (r) ∀t ≥ t0 (r).
   62 
   63 The graphs and weights may depend on t, but not on r.
   64     A complementary fixed-graph obstruction has the opposite quantifier order. No fixed finite
   65 weighted structure is a strict dB amplifier for every r > 1: every structure not dB-equivalent to the
   66 complete graph is suppressing above a graph-dependent threshold RG , whereas the equivalent class
   67 ties the baseline (Kriebel, 2026a). This ∀G ∃RG statement supplies no graph-uniform threshold
   68 and therefore does not rule out the present ∃{Gt } ∀r < R ∃t0 (r) order; the thresholds RGt may
   69 vary with t.
   70     Svoboda et al. (2024) constructed simultaneous amplifiers on 1 < r < 1.2. A preliminary unref-
   71 ereed research release established Rsim ≥ 3/2 with a center–triangle family, while that particular
   72 family suppresses at its endpoint (Kriebel, 2026b). The present manuscript supersedes that release
   73 in its lower-bound conclusion by crossing 3/2. The numerical increment is small, but the conclusion
   74 is qualitative: the earlier endpoint is not the unrestricted phase boundary.
   75     The proof has four ingredients. First, a finite Schur complement gives the rare-migration trace
   76 without an independence or branching approximation. Second, stopped birth–death comparisons
   77 control establishment and the entire path to fixation at the smaller q/C scale of the final gain. Third,
   78 the exact two-coordinate macro chain retains adverse reversals while deriving the pair gate. Finally,
   79 optimizing the two resulting response functions produces an algebraic tangency and the degree-six
   80 polynomial in the abstract. This also proves fixed-parameter optimality within the displayed first-
   81 order pair–pendant response model. No universal upper bound is inferred; the unrestricted value
   82 of Rsim remains open.
