  201 Proof. Work over the algebraic number field K = Q(Rhyb ). Both σ∗ and λ∗ lie in K, and mt = bλ∗ tc
  202 is obtained by an exact comparison of real algebraic numbers. For fixed t, e and either update rule,
  203 the transient first-step system of Gt (2−e ) has entries in K(r). Cramer’s rule therefore puts its
  204 fixation probability in K(r); the same is true of the finite separated trace and the complete-graph
  205 baseline. For each r ∈ It , every transient block Q(r) occurring in these absorbing systems is
  206 substochastic with spectral radius less than one. Hence I − Q(r) is a nonsingular M -matrix and
  207 det(I − Q(r)) > 0; the event denominators are positive as well.
  208     After clearing those positive denominators, each of the two absolute-value conditions defining
