 1076 8    Discussion
 1077 The central contribution is the quantifier in Theorem 3. One weighted graph sequence is chosen
 1078 without knowing fitness and works for each fixed r in an interval extending beyond 3/2. A family
 1079 tuned separately at each fitness would establish a weaker statement. The diagonal choice of εt is
 1080 therefore part of the construction, not merely a technical limit: compact convergence selects one
 1081 positive cut that works simultaneously on an expanding interval of fitnesses.
 1082     The mechanism uses two modules with opposite response signatures. Heavy pairs contribute
 1083 positively to dB and negatively to Bd, whereas hub pendants contribute positively to Bd and
 1084 negatively to dB. Their densities are vanishing, so the well-mixed core supplies the baseline and
 1085 the module responses appear at the common scale qt /Ct . The exact Schur trace is important here.
 1086 It resolves every local excursion before the next weak-cut event and retains the possibility that a
 1087 nearly successful global sweep reverses; replacing it by an independent-lineage approximation would
 1088 not prove fixation.
 1089     Biologically, Bd and dB idealize two different ways local turnover can be resolved: reproduction
 1090 can select a parent before a replacement site, or a vacancy can first determine which neighbors
 1091 compete. The construction shows that robustness to this order need not require most individuals to
 1092 
 1093 
 1094                                                   19
 1095 occupy a special environment. A vanishing fraction of paired niches and hub-associated peripheral
 1096 sites can bias invasion in complementary ways while the bulk remains well mixed. The result is
 1097 qualitative rather than a claim of empirical optimality: it identifies a mechanism by which rare
 1098 structural heterogeneities can overcome update-order antagonism in this beneficial-fitness regime.
 1099      There are two sharp limitations. Proposition 15 optimizes only fixed positive (σ, λ) in the dis-
 1100 played first-order dilute response model. It does not classify size-dependent or singular choices
 1101 σt , λt , different pair or pendant density scales, second-order gains on a vanishing first-order bound-
 1102 ary, nonseparated pair dynamics, other modules, or non-dilute interactions. Conversely, the paper
 1103 supplies no finite universal upper bound on Rsim . Establishing such a bound would require a
 1104 comparison that couples Bd and dB fixation on arbitrary growing weighted graphs, rather than a
 1105 classification of this construction.
 1106                                                                                             −3/4
 1107      The quantitative advantage is also asymptotically small. Here qt /Ct = t−3 = Θ(nt           ), so the
 1108 normalized fixation gain tends to zero. The pair weights grow like Ct , while the exact diagonal
 1109 may select very small positive weak edges. The proof gives eventual positivity for each fixed fitness
 1110 but no useful finite-size bound on t0 (r), and it makes no claim of empirical or finite-population
 1111 optimality.
