   737	 =\inf\{s>\sigma_j:\ell_s\ne\ell_{\sigma_j}\}\wedge\tau_\uparrow.
   738	\]
   739	Assign the stopped trace the level
   740	$\widehat\ell_j=\ell_{\sigma_j}$ when $\sigma_j<\tau_\uparrow$ and the level
   741	$\widehat\ell_j=m$ when $\sigma_j=\tau_\uparrow$, and keep it at $m$
   742	thereafter.  Put $N=\inf\{j:\widehat\ell_j=m\}$.  The stopped committor
   743	comparison above gives a fixed $\beta>1$ such that, on $\{j<N\}$, the
   744	conditional up-or-exit/down odds of $\widehat\ell$ are at least $\beta$; at
   745	$\ell=0$ a downward move is impossible.  Thus, with
   746	$\varepsilon=(\beta-1)/(\beta+1)>0$,
   747	$\widehat\ell_{j\wedge N}-\varepsilon(j\wedge N)$ is a submartingale.
   748	Optional stopping at $N\wedge k$ gives
   749	\[
   750	 \varepsilon\,\mathbb E(N\wedge k)
   751	 \leq\mathbb E\widehat\ell_{N\wedge k}-\ell_0\leq m,
   752	\]
   753	and monotone convergence gives $\mathbb EN\leq m/\varepsilon=O(m)$.
   754	
   755	It remains to convert stopped trace changes into calendar time.  Before
   756	$\tau_\uparrow$, during an $h=0$ phase the activation-or-loss intensity is
   757	bounded below by a positive constant, and during an $h=1$ phase with
   758	$\ell<m$ the deactivation-or-gain intensity is at least
   759	$m-\ell\geq1$.  Moreover,
   760	\begin{align*}
   761	 \Prob(\text{loss or upper exit before activation}\mid h=0)
   762	 &\geq \frac{V_\ell}{r_+(1+\ell)+V_\ell}\geq\frac{c_0}{C},
   763	 &&1\leq\ell<m,\\
   764	 \Prob(\text{gain or upper exit before deactivation}\mid h=1)
   765	 &\geq\frac{\underline U_\ell}
   766	 {2\delta+m-\ell+\underline U_\ell}\geq\frac{c_0}{C},
   767	 &&0\leq\ell<m.
   768	\end{align*}
   769	At $\ell=0$ a resident-hub phase has no loss, but it ends by activation (or
   770	upper exit), after which the mutant-hub bound applies.  Core jumps contribute
   771	zero to functions of $(h,\ell)$ and do not pause these competing clocks.
   772	Consequently, grouping at most two consecutive hub phases, the number of
   773	phases before the next pendant change or upper-strip exit has conditional mean
   774	$O(C)$, and every phase has uniformly bounded conditional mean duration.
   775	Thus, on $\{j<N\}$,
   776	\begin{equation}
   777	 \mathbb E(\sigma_{j+1}-\sigma_j\mid\mathcal F_{\sigma_j})
   778	 \leq K_0C,
   779	\label{eq:leaf-wait}
   780	\end{equation}
   781	and summing over $j<N$ yields
   782	$\mathbb E\xi\leq K_0C\,\mathbb EN=O(Cm)$.  If initially
   783	$(h,\ell)=(0,m)$, activation, pendant loss, or upper-strip exit occurs in
   784	bounded mean time.  The probability that loss occurs first is $O(C^{-1})$;
   785	activation or exit stops the synchronization, while after a loss the preceding
   786	trace starts from $m-1$.  Therefore, for
   787	\[
   788	 \Sigma=\inf\{s\geq0:(h_s,\ell_s)=(1,m)
   789	                         \text{ or }R_s\geq2\delta c\},
   790	\]
   791	\begin{equation}
   792	 \sup_{\substack{0\leq R<2\delta c,\ h\in\{0,1\}\\0\leq\ell\leq m}}
   793	 \mathbb E_{h,R,\ell}\Sigma=O(Cm).
   794	\label{eq:leaf-hitting-time}
   795	\end{equation}
   796	
   797	On the nonexceptional event $\{\Sigma<\tau_\uparrow\}$ the process is at
   798	$(h,\ell)=(1,m)$.  First suppress hub loss and keep $\ell=m$.  The Bd deficit
   799	generator is then exactly
   800	\[
   801	 \mathcal G R
   802	 =-R\left\{\frac r{c+m}+(r-1)\left(1-\frac Rc\right)\right\}.
   803	\]
   804	After shrinking $\delta$ once, the bracket is at least a compact-uniform
   805	$\kappa>0$ on $R\leq3\delta c$.  Starting from $R\leq2\delta c$ and stopping
   806	at $\tau=\tau_0\wedge\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}}$, Dynkin's formula gives
   807	\[
   808	 \mathbb E\int_0^\tau R_s\,ds\leq\frac{R(0)}\kappa
   809	 \leq\frac{2\delta c}\kappa.
   810	\]
   811	The same adapted step calculation, now with $3\delta$ in place of
   812	$2\delta$, also gives
   813	$\Prob(\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}}<\tau_0)\leq e^{-\gamma C}$.  Conditional
   814	on the suppressed path, the probability of no hub loss is
   815	$\exp\{-\int_0^\tau R_s/c\,ds\}$.  Jensen's inequality and subtraction of
   816	the upper-exit event therefore yield
   817	\begin{equation}
   818	 \Prob(\hbox{no hub loss and }\tau_0<\tau_{\{R\geq3\delta c\}})
   819	 \geq e^{-2\delta/\kappa}-e^{-\gamma C}=:s_1+o(1)>0.
   820	\label{eq:Bd-final-cleanup}
   821	\end{equation}
   822	The same exponential Lyapunov estimate makes this final cleanup occur within
   823	$C^2$ with probability $1-o(1)$.  Together with
   824	\eqref{eq:leaf-hitting-time}, $m=o(C)$, Markov's inequality, and
   825	\eqref{eq:core-confinement}, this gives a fixed $s_0>0$ chance of full cleanup
   826	in a block of $2C^2$ time.  If hub loss ends
   827	an unsuccessful final attempt, the same adapted deficit comparison, now
   828	using the nested strips $2\delta c<3\delta c<4\delta c$ after shrinking
   829	$\delta$, returns the process to $R\leq\delta c$ in polynomial time before
   830	the next block; escape to $4\delta c$ before that return costs
   831	$e^{-\gamma C}$.  Starting the next block from $R\leq\delta c$ puts it back
   832	under the $2\delta c$ confinement estimate.  The earlier
   833	hit of $3\delta c$ in \eqref{eq:Bd-final-cleanup} is charged to this same
   834	exceptional event rather than recycled as a new block.  We now formalize the
   835	repetition.
   836	
